 ## Overview
-Compressive Gradient Rendering is a compressive sensing technique that renders discrete gradient components instead of pixel values to exploit sparsity in the gradient domain, then reconstructs the image using a Poisson solver [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard volume rendering by optimizing for gradient coefficients in the frequency domain rather than direct pixel intensities, allowing for reconstruction from fewer samples than the Nyquist rate [S1, unofficial].
+Compressive Gradient Rendering is a compressive sensing technique that renders discrete gradient components instead of pixel values to exploit sparsity in the Fourier domain, then reconstructs the image using a Poisson solver [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard Compressive Volume Rendering by operating on gradient fields rather than intensity values, allowing for higher recovery rates with fewer samples by leveraging the sparsity of image derivatives [S1, unofficial].
 
